{{also|Alliteration|allitération}}
==English==
{{Webster 1913}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|NL.|en}} {{m|la|alliteratio}}, from {{m|la|alliteratus}}, from {{m|la|allitero}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|ad||to, towards, near}} and {{m|la|litera||a letter}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/əˌlɪtəˈɹeɪʃən/|[əˌlɪɾəˈɹeɪʃən]|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|eɪʃən|lang=en}}
* {{audio|En-ca-alliteration.oga|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

{{examples-right|sense=repetition of initial consonants|examples=''Behemoth, biggest born of earth, upheaved His vastness.'' - {{w|Milton}}.<br>''Fly o'er waste fens and windy fields.'' -{{w|Alfred Tennyson}}}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The repetition of sounds at the beginning of two or more words immediately succeeding each other, or at short intervals.
#*'''2018''' (March 20) [http://www.itv.com/news/border/2018-03-20/fish-fury-flares-over-brussels-brexit-deal/ Fish fury flares over Brussels Brexit deal] ''ITV''
#*:So fish fury all round, as there has been in the past. And as an aside, that '''alliteration''' was, sadly, not mine that of a former political correspondent of the Daily Record, John Deans, and applied to the 'cod wars' with Iceland.
# The recurrence of the same letter in accented parts of words, as in Anglo-Saxon alliterative meter.
{{examples-right|sense=repetition of consonants in accented word parts|examples=''In a somer seson whan soft was the sonne, I shope me in shroudes as I a shepe were.'' - {{w|Piers Plowman}}.}}

